ustment": -0.69, "key_factors": ["Open seat in an R-leaning district (PVI R+4) with a Democratic-tilting national environment", "Expert ratings split: mostly Tossup, with two raters calling it a Republican flip and two leaning Democratic", "Thin, dated polling (last real survey late August) shows a dead heat, all Democratic-sponsored", "Even fundraising on both sides; Dandiya holds a slight cash edge"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point slightly Republican given the district's R+4 lean, but the favorable national environment, even money, and a late-August poll showing a tied race pull it back to even. Expert ratings are split between Tossup and modest tilts in both directions, so a near-zero Democratic edge with wide uncertainty fits the evidence.", "watch": "Any independent-sponsored polling in October, or movement in the housing-crisis story around Askar's rentals, could break the tie either way.", "confidence": "low", "notes": []}, "overview": "Florida's 22nd District is an open seat where Republican Casey Askar and Democrat Pia Dandiya are running even in money and, so far, in the polls. The district leans Republican by about four points, but the national environment favors Democrats this year, offsetting that structural disadvantage. Expert raters mostly call it a Tossup, though a couple see a Republican flip and two lean Democratic.\n\nThe available polling is sparse and dated, with the last live survey from late August showing a tie, and all surveys come from Democratic-leaning sponsors. Prediction markets price the race narrowly in Dandiya's favor. With no incumbent and both candidates well funded, this remains one of the closest House races on the map, with genuine two-sided risk.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.5161, "margin": 0.22}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.4293, "margin": -1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.5493, "margin": 0.2}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.5402, "margin": 0.2}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.4658